WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.743
Python is popular because it is readable, flexible, expressive, and
supported by a huge ecosystem.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:11.554
Those strengths help teams build useful software quickly, but they can
also hide risky assumptions.

3
00:00:11.554 --> 00:00:14.288
A small script can become a production service.

4
00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:17.296
A notebook experiment can become an automation job.

5
00:00:17.296 --> 00:00:20.167
A copied example can become a long-lived pattern.

6
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:28.303
Security mistakes often appear when code grows faster than the engineering
decisions around trust, ownership, configuration, and review.

7
00:00:28.303 --> 00:00:33.498
It is useful to separate a bug, a weakness, a vulnerability, and an
insecure design choice.

8
00:00:33.498 --> 00:00:35.139
A bug is incorrect behavior.

9
00:00:35.139 --> 00:00:39.241
A weakness is a pattern that can create risk under the right conditions.

10
00:00:39.241 --> 00:00:43.890
A vulnerability is a weakness that can be used to violate a security
expectation.

11
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.949
An insecure design choice may work as implemented but still create
avoidable exposure.

12
00:00:48.949 --> 00:00:57.221
These categories overlap, but the distinction helps teams respond with the
right fix instead of treating every issue as the same kind of defect.

13
00:00:57.221 --> 00:01:10.825
Common causes include trusting input too early, relying on permissive
defaults, copying snippets without understanding their assumptions,
skipping clear ownership, and allowing development shortcuts to survive
into production.

14
00:01:10.825 --> 00:01:17.798
Python makes it easy to connect files, APIs, databases, shells, templates,
queues, parsers, and third-party packages.

15
00:01:17.798 --> 00:01:23.473
Each connection is useful, but each connection also creates a place where
assumptions can change.

16
00:01:23.473 --> 00:01:35.505
Most mistakes appear at boundaries: user requests, uploaded files, API
messages, database queries, command execution, templates, package
dependencies, configuration, environment variables, and logs.

17
00:01:35.505 --> 00:01:37.556
The goal of this course is not blame.

18
00:01:37.556 --> 00:01:40.427
The goal is repeatable engineering improvement.

19
00:01:40.427 --> 00:01:51.160
When teams can recognize common patterns, choose safer APIs, test negative
cases, and review decisions explicitly, Python applications become easier
to secure and easier to maintain.
